      "text": "Preservation of Revelation\nwritten by five scribes on parchment in the Ḥijāzī script—an early, slanted Arabic\nscript characteristically lacking vowels and diacritical markings. It is remarkably\nclose to the modern standard text of the Quran, with the underlying consonantal\ntext (the rasm) verified to be virtually identical to the standard 'Uthmanic tradition\nused today, differing only in minor surface details due to early orthography, scribal\nerrors, and regional variant readings (qirāʾāt).39\n\nṢanʿāʾ Collection (7th – early 8th CE)\nThe Ṣanʿāʾ cache discovered during restoration of the Great Mosque of Ṣanʿāʾ in\n1972 contained thousands of Qur'anic manuscript fragments. The famous Ṣanʿāʾ 1 /\nDAM 01-27.140 used in textual-critical arguments is one particular manuscript that\nhas generated significant interest in recent years. It is a palimpsest41 and studies show\nthat the parchment dates to the 6-7th century CE with two textual layers:42\n   •   Ṣanʿāʾ 1 lower text (C-1 / scriptio inferior)\n        o   Earlier Qur'anic writing on the parchment — later erased\n        o  Date Range: 578–669 CE (at a 95% confidence interval)\n        o   Significantly non-standard textual profile\n    •   Ṣanʿāʾ 1 upper text (scriptio superior)\n        o  Subsequently written over the erased parchment\n        o  Date Range: 7th - early 8th century CE\n        o  Belongs to the standard consonantal Qur'anic tradition.\nIt is the lower text which has generated a lot of controversy in recent times.\nPolemicists have used findings from recent historical critical scholarship to make an",
