      "text": "Reason, Revelation and the Architecture of Truth\ninference that the standard Qur’an possessed today evolved from an earlier text. But\nhere we are only interested in what the actual evidence shows.\nIf Ṣanʿāʾ Lower preserves an earlier developmental Qur'an, we should find some\ncoherent direction that would provide evidence of evolution such as theological\nclarification, legal development, cancellation and replacement of command, or\nanything that would tell us that the Qur’an was in a state of evolution.\nWhen a directionality test is run, there is no coherent editorial evolution from Ṣanʿāʾ\nLower → standard Qur’an. Instead, we note a noisy-copy/transmission profile.\nSadeghi identifies auto-contamination as one of the mechanisms — wording from\nelsewhere in the Qurʾān interfering with the scribe’s reproduction of the text from\nmemory.43\n    Their conclusion is not that Ṣanʿāʾ preserves some radically different Qur'an.\nThey say the opposite: Ṣanʿāʾ Lower and the standard Qur'an are generally the same\nand the differences are the exception rather than the rule. More importantly, they\nargue that the two texts are sister branches from a common source (𝑷)— not that\nlower text serves as an ancestral prototype.\n    Thus, Sanʿāʾ Lower has no demonstrated continuing manuscript lineage, and\nindependent early codices corroborate the standard rasm. There is no evidence of an\nevolving Qur’an similar to what we find in the textual genealogies of Judeo-\nChristian scriptures. There is no older theology being replaced, no legal\ndevelopment emerges, no doctrinal correction appears and no competing Qur'an\nevolves from the lower text.\n\n43 Sadeghi and Bergmann (2010), pp. 387–405; and Behnam Sadeghi and Mohsen Goudarzi,\n“Ṣan‘ā’ 1 and the Origins of the Qur’ān,” Der Islam 87 (2012): 1–129.\n                                59",
