      "text": "The Qurʾān: a Philosopher’s Toolkit\n1.  The Qurʾānic Assertion\n    Across the text, the Qurʾān issues continuous calls to think:\n    “Do they not reflect?” (Q 6:50; 7:184; 23:68)\n    “Do you not reason?” (Q 2:44; 2:76; 36:62; 23:80)\n    “That they may reflect.” (Q 59:21)\n    “Do they not ponder the Qurʾān?” (Q 4:82; 47:24)\n   These verses are not rhetorical flourishes. They define the Qurʾān’s\nepistemological posture:\n    •   Reason is required.\n    •   Reflection is encouraged.\n    •   Pondering the text is a moral duty.\n    •   Understanding is not blindly inherited; it is achieved.\n  Q 4:82 is especially decisive:\n    “Do they not ponder the Qurʾān? Had it been from other than God, they\n    would have found in it much contradiction.” (Q 4:82)\n    This verse links reflection to verification—the very essence of the CCC model.\nThe intellect is not optional equipment; it is the instrument through which truth\nitself is confirmed.\n\n2.  Rational Inference\n  A human founder with an agenda for seeking authority is unlikely to:\n    •   Encourage open-ended questioning.\n    •  Demand reflection (Kahneman’s system 2 thinking).\n    •   Challenge readers to analyze the text for coherence.\n    •   Invite scrutiny that could undermine his claims.\n   Human power-seeking systems fear the consequences of reflective thought. The\nQurʾān depends on them. It is difficult to imagine a human author commanding\nreaders to test the text for internal consistency (Q 4:82) and ponder its meaning (Q\n\n                                110",
