      "text": "Reason, Revelation and the Architecture of Truth\n47:24). But a text confident in its truth invites reflection because reflection confirms\nit.\n       Category            Human Systems                 Qurʾānic Revelation\n Attitude toward        Discourage to retain control            Encourage to reveal truth\n questioning\n Structure of belief       Authority-based                     Wisdom-based\n Expected behavior      Compliance                         Independent reflection\n Treatment of doubt     Suppress                            Engage\n Risk tolerance        Low — questioning undermines       High — truth welcomes\n                      power                                   scrutiny\n\n   The Qurʾān relies on reason. This is not the logic of a human power structure; it\nis the confidence of a text that expects truth to withstand examination.\n\n3.  Philosophical Significance\n    This pillar expresses the Qurʾān’s epistemic ideal:\n\n     Qurʾānic Principle           Philosophical Parallel                 Implication\n  Reflect, think, reason        Rational epistemology           Knowledge emerges through\n                                                                inquiry\n Ponder the text             Hermeneutics, pan-textual        Understanding requires effort\n                                 analysis\n Reflection produces clarity   Virtue epistemology            The mind must be exercised\n Reason as moral duty       Moral psychology                    Intellect is part of accountability\n\n   The Qurʾān treats use of the intellect as a moral act. This human faculty is not a\nthreat to revelation; it is the key to unlock it. Where postmodernism collapses truth\ninto language and power, and scientism collapses meaning into measurement, the\nQurʾān insists that the intellect is the bridge between revelation and reality.",
