      "text": "Reason, Revelation and the Architecture of Truth\n\n   Dimension                Anthropocentric                         Qurʾān\n\n                     try to “make the world better,” and\n                 measure success by pleasure and progress\n\n Soteriology    No objective salvation or final             Repentance, purification of the soul,\n                    reconciliation beyond death. Yet people     remembrance, divine light, and\n                   seek therapy, pursue “healing,” practice     return to God (Q 39:53; 91:9–10;\n                   mindfulness, long for redemption arcs,80    89:27–30)\n                and talk about “becoming the best version\n                   of myself.”\n\n Eschatology    No guaranteed final, cosmic justice          Resurrection, full unveiling of\n                beyond death. Yet people fight for justice,   deeds, and perfect justice; every\n               demand fairness, rage at unpunished        atom’s weight is accounted for (Q\n                 wrongdoing, seek closure, and call for       99:1–8; 36:51–54; 3:185)\n                      historical accountability\n\n    Hence, in summary, the two views can be contrasted along the following\ndimensions as shown in table 6.2. This comparison examines the underlying logic of\neach worldview, not the personal beliefs, intentions, or moral character of\nindividuals.\n\nConclusion and Final Thoughts\n    In the early part of the chapter, we alluded to the decoupling of humanity from\nits desire to pursue truth and attainment of higher ideals, to a reign of quantity\npredicated on worldly desires (Q 30:7). The Qurʾān provides the answer in the next\nverse:\n    “Have they not reflected upon themselves? God created not the heavens and\n     the earth except in truth (haqq) and for an appointed term.” (Q 30:8)",
