      "text": "Reason, Revelation and the Architecture of Truth\n    “Those who disbelieved say, ‘This is nothing but a lie he invented\n     (iftarāhu), and other people have helped him (aʿānahu) in it.’” (Q 25:4)\n    Here, the Qurʾān itself prepares the reader for Q 25:5 by juxtaposing two\ndistinct verb patterns—Form VIII and Form IV—in a single verse. This\nmorphological contrast provides an internal linguistic clue.\n    •       اِفْتَرَاهُ    (iftarāhu) — Form VIII from the root * ي ر ف (f-r-y, “to fabricate”).\n    → Means “He fabricated it himself.”\n    → The action originates within the subject. (Reflexive / self-initiated)\n    •      أَعَانَهُ    (aʿānahu) — Form IV from * ن و ع(ʿ-w-n, “to help”).\n    → Means “They helped him.”\n    → Other agents participate in the action. Assisted / transitive)\n    In one verse, the Qurʾān employs both forms side-by-side to distinguish two\nkinds of agency:\n    •   self-initiated action (Form VIII), and\n    •   assisted action (Form IV).\n    This internal contrast showcases the Qurʾān’s morphological precision—when\nGod intends a causative meaning, He employs Form IV (or Form II); when He\nintends a self-directed action, He employs Form VIII.\n\nForm VIII Verbs in the Qurʾān: Morphological Boundaries of Agency\n\n     A survey of all 963 Form VIII verbs occurring for 101 roots in the Qurʾān\ndemonstrates a broadly consistent semantic pattern: none of them denote delegated\nperformance by another agent. This pattern is not incidental but structural. The\nQurʾān’s internal verb system distinguishes sharply between Forms IV, II (causative)\nand VIII (reflexive). That consistency lends decisive grammatical weight to the\nreflexive reading of (Q 25:5).\n    Table E2 summarizes representative examples:\n\n                                225",
