      "text": "Appendices\n\nTable E2\nForm VIII reflexive verbs found in the Qurʾān\n\n Form VIII verb Occurrences      Semantic function     Someone else performs the action?\n\n  اِتَّقَى (ittaqā)     166        Guard oneself; exercise restraint No\n\n َاِتَّبَع (ittabaʿa)    136         Follow                No\n\n َاِتَّخَذ (ittakhadha) 124         Take, adopt, appoint        No\n\n اِفْتَرَى (iftarā)    50            Fabricate, invent          No\n\n اِهْتَدَى (ihtadā)   40          Find or follow guidance      No\n\n َاِخْتَلَف (ikhtalafa) 35            Differ, disagree           No\n\n اِبْتَغَى (ibtaghā)   34           Seek, pursue             No\n\n اِشْتَرَى (ishtarā)  21          Buy, exchange            No\n\n َاِقْتَتَل (iqtatala)   4            Fight one another               Reciprocal, not delegated\n\n َاِكْتَتَب (iktataba)  1          Wrote down / had written down Disputed: 25:5\n\n   While iktatabah is only mentioned once, other Form VIII (iftaʿala) verbs\nuniformly express self-directed or reciprocal action; none function causatively\ndelegating action to another person. This consistent internal morphology confirms\nthat iktatabah—“he wrote them down for himself”—rather than any causative\nprocurement (“he had them written”). The Qurʾān’s own morphology attributes\nauthorship and agency to him directly, not to delegated scribes. The contrast\nbetween iftarāhu (Form VIII, Q 25:4) and aʿānahu (Form IV, same verse) confirms\nthis morphological boundary.\n\n                                226",
