      "text": "Reason, Revelation and the Architecture of Truth\nQ 80:13–15 “In honored pages, in the hands of scribes”\n         بَرَرَةٍ       كِرَامٍ سَفَرَةٍ  بِأَيْدِي  مُّطَهَّرَةٍ فُوعَةٍمَّرْ  مُّكَرَّمَةٍ صُحُفٍ فِي\n     Fī ṣuḥufin mukarramah, marfūʿatin muṭahharah, bi-aydī safarah\n    kirāmin bararah.\n    In honored pages, exalted and purified, by the hands of scribes—noble and\n     righteous.” (Q 80:13–15)\n    The revelation is portrayed as ṣuḥuf—“written pages” or “scrolls”—handled by\nsafarah, meaning scribes. The term safarah derives from the triliteral root ر - ف- س (s-\nf-r), which in classical Arabic denotes “to uncover, to make manifest, to bring to\nlight.”\n    As Lane’s Arabic–English Lexicon notes:106 “َسَفَر — He uncovered, revealed, or\nmade manifest a thing that was concealed; hence ِّفْرالس (book) is so called because it\ndiscloses or makes manifest knowledge written therein.”\n    This linguistic background shows that sāfir—and its plural safarah—describes\nthose who make revelation manifest, i.e., scribes who inscribe divine communication\nin written form.\n    Hence the verse reinforces revelation as a written and textual phenomenon, not\nmerely an oral recital. It depicts a process of transmission involving scribes of high\nmoral standing, suggesting that the Qurʾān was being written down, copied, and\npreserved from its earliest phase of revelation, ready for dissemination among the\nbelievers.\n\nQ 68:1 and Q 96:4 — “By the Pen”\nThe vivid imagery described in Q 68:1 and Q 96:4 indirectly provides further\nevidence of a writing culture within the community that lacked exposure to any\nprior revelation.\n         َيَسْطُرُون مَاوَ وِلْقَلَم ٱ َ  نٓ\n     Nūn. Wa-al-qalami wa-mā yasṭurūn.\n\n106 Lane’s Arabic–English Lexicon (vol. 1, p. 1413)\n                                229",
