      "text": "Who break the covenant of Allāh after contracting it and \nsever that which Allāh has ordered to be joined and cause \ncorruption on earth[...] \n(2:27)\n\nI have no way of definitively establishing the difference between \nfisq and fusūq. The Traditionalist has none that I have seen based \nin Qur’anic principles, and he renders the two terms as the same \nthing without discernible principles.\n\nAll instances in the text appear in the notes.\n\nReferences\n\nverb\n2:59, 6:49, 7:163, 7:165, 10:33, 17:16, 18:50, 29:34, 32:20, \n46:20.\n\nnoun\n2:197, 2:282, 5:3, 6:121, 6:145, 49:7, 49:11.\n\nactive participle (noun)\n2:26, 2:99, 3:82, 3:110, 5:47, 5:49, 5:59, 5:81, 7:102, 7:145, 9:8, \n9:24, 9:67, 9:84, 24:4, 24:55, 32:18, 46:35, 49:6, 57:16, 57:26, \n57:27, 59:5, 59:19.\n\nactive participle (adjective)\n5:25, 5:26, 5:108, 9:53, 9:80, 9:96, 21:74, 27:12, 28:32, 43:54, \n51:46, 61:5, 63:6.\n\nXI.viii\nḥ-n-th ­— perjury\n\nThe meaning of this root in Arabic is to perjure oneself. The root \noccurs twice in the text and I have rendered both according to \nthe root meaning.\n\nBoth instances in the text appear in the notes.\n\nReferences\n38:44, 56:46.\n\nXI.ix\nsayyi’ah — evil",
