      "topics": [],
      "text": "After comparing all instances of law lā in the text I came to the \nconclusion that where law lā occurs as a single statement (as \nabove) it indicates a wish couched in doubt. In the mouths of \nwaverers, rejecters of God, dishonest scribes and the like, it \nallows for a range of moods: that of excuse, a challenge with \nlittle chance of being met, a nit-picking wistfulness and the \ninsinuation of blame levelled at another. In the mouth of God it \nexpresses a light challenge or rebuke, a prod. In the mouth of a \ngood man it expresses a godly regret or rebuke.\n\n1\t\nQur’anic verses in italics are from The Qur’an – Arabic Text with \nCorresponding English Meanings by Saheeh International.\n\n[...]Oh that God would but speak to us or a sign but come \nto us!\n\nAll instances in the text appear in the notes.\n\nlaw lā (part of double statement)\n2:64, 2:251, 4:83, 4:113, 7:43, 8:68, 9:122, 10:19, 11:91, 11:110, \n12:24, 12:94, 17:74, 20:129, 22:40, 24:10, 24:14, 24:20, 24:21, \n25:42, 25:77, 28:10, 28:82, 29:53, 34:31, 37:57, 37:143, 41:45, \n42:14, 42:21, 43:33, 48:25, 59:3, 63:10, 68:49.",
